{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The loading bar that froze the instant the app got busy</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A progress indicator slides left to right by animating its <code>left</code> CSS property. It looks smooth in isolation. The moment the app does real work on the main thread, parsing a large response, running a heavy computation, the bar visibly stutters or freezes mid-slide, then jumps to where it should be once the work finishes. Nothing about the animation code changed; what changed is that <code>left</code> can't move without the browser recomputing layout and repainting, and both of those need the same main thread the heavy work is currently occupying.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Not every CSS property costs the same to animate. Properties like <code>width</code>, <code>height</code>, <code>top</code>, and <code>left</code> sit at the "layout" stage of the browser's rendering pipeline, changing them can shift where every other element on the page needs to be, so the browser has to redo layout, then paint, then composite, every single frame. <code>transform</code> and <code>opacity</code> don't affect any other element's position or size; the browser can skip layout and paint for them entirely and hand the change straight to compositing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The sharper version of the same fact: transform and opacity changes can be handled entirely by the compositor thread, a thread separate from the main thread that's running your application's JavaScript. That's not just "cheaper," it's structurally independent, a compositor-only animation can keep running smoothly during a stretch where the main thread is fully blocked, because it was never waiting on the main thread to produce each frame in the first place.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Layout properties re-run the whole pipeline; transform/opacity skip straight to compositing</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The browser turns a frame into pixels through a few stages: layout (where does everything go), paint (turn each element into pixels), and composite (combine the painted layers onto the screen). Changing <code>left</code> or <code>width</code> means the browser doesn't know where anything ends up until it reruns layout, so it has to do all three stages, every frame, for a smooth animation. Changing <code>transform: translateX(...)</code> instead moves an already-painted layer around without asking layout or paint to run again, only the last, cheapest stage, compositing, has to happen.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      This is often summarized as "stick to compositor-only properties": restrict animations to <code>transform</code> and <code>opacity</code> wherever the visual result can be achieved that way, a slide is a <code>translate</code>, a resize-in-place is a <code>scale</code>, a fade is <code>opacity</code>. The payoff isn't marginal, it's the difference between a pipeline stage that's proportional to the whole page's layout complexity and one that's a fixed, cheap operation regardless of how much else is on the page.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The compositor-thread independence is the part worth internalizing precisely: because transform/opacity animations don't require the main thread to compute each frame, they keep running even while the main thread is busy with something else entirely, a long synchronous computation, a big JSON parse, expensive React reconciliation. A layout-property animation has no such independence; every frame update is main-thread work competing with whatever else the main thread is doing, and under load it simply loses frames.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The onboarding animation that was smooth in dev and janky for real users</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team built a slide-in onboarding tooltip animated by changing its <code>left</code> position across the screen. In local development, on an idle page, it looked perfectly smooth. In production, real-user monitoring showed it stuttering for a meaningful share of users, specifically ones where the tooltip appeared while the app was also fetching and rendering initial data, exactly the moment the main thread was busiest and least able to keep recomputing layout every frame.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The fix was mechanical once identified: swap the animation from changing <code>left</code> to changing <code>transform: translateX(...)</code>, same visual motion, same distance, same duration. The stutter disappeared under the exact same production conditions that had caused it, because the animation no longer needed the main thread to compute each frame at all, it just needed the compositor thread, which wasn't contending with the data-fetch work for anything.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      What made this easy to miss in development was that the bug was load-dependent, not code-dependent, the animation code was never "wrong" in isolation, it only degraded under exactly the kind of main-thread contention that a quiet local dev environment doesn't reproduce. The team's later process fix was a lint rule flagging <code>transition</code>/<code>animation</code> declarations targeting layout-triggering properties, catching the pattern at write-time instead of relying on production RUM to surface it after the fact.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

