{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>will-change, layer cost, and knowing which properties are safe</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      <code>will-change: transform</code> can hint to the browser that an element is about to animate, letting it prepare a compositor layer ahead of time instead of on the first frame of the animation. It's a targeted tool, not a default to sprinkle everywhere, applying it to too many elements creates too many layers, which costs memory and can itself slow things down.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Not every visual change has a compositor-only equivalent. A layout reflow that genuinely needs to push other elements around, an accordion expanding and shoving content below it down, can't be faked with <code>transform</code> alone; the honest options are accepting the layout cost for that specific interaction, or restructuring so the expanding content overlays instead of displacing (which <em>can</em> be transform-driven).
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Before reaching for any property outside <code>transform</code>/<code>opacity</code> in a hot animation path, check what pipeline stage it actually triggers, some properties assumed to be "layout" are actually paint-only (like <code>background-color</code> or <code>box-shadow</code>, cheaper than layout but still not compositor-only), and some genuinely require layout no matter how they're written. <code>will-change</code> should be applied via JavaScript just before the animation starts and removed after, not left permanently in a stylesheet, a layer that exists indefinitely for an element that's animating rarely is pure memory overhead with no offsetting benefit.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

